{
 "source_id": "declarations/pfa-log-club-exceptions",
 "_this_is_not_a_source": "A declaration. Nothing here is fetched; every value below was printed by Pro Football Archives and is kept as printed on the claims it names.",
 "_what": "Game-log rows held on a club OTHER than the one their full name prints, by Ryan's ruling. Named row by row. There is no general rule here about full names: the rule stays that a game row's club is its link and full name (2026-09-11), and each exception is an instance with its evidence.",
 "_how_it_is_held": "src/ingest_pfa_logs.py holds a declared row on `held_on_club_id` / `held_on_club_string` ONLY while the row still prints exactly `full_name_as_printed`, `short_label_as_printed` and `opponent_as_printed`; otherwise the rule applies and the row is counted as no longer as declared. Every held claim carries `club_held_by_declared_exception`.",
 "_gated_not_exempted": "src/gate_game_club.py G4: every declared row is in the model, marked, on its declared club, and STILL shows its declared evidence -- the full name and the label as declared, and the opponents' rows in that game still printing `opponents_print`. G5: the rows of this shape in the whole archive are EXACTLY the declared rows. If PFA corrects these pages, G4 fails and says the exception has become wrong; it never silently overrides a source that now agrees with itself.",
 "exceptions": [
  {
   "id": "pfa-1934-cincinnati-reds-st-louis-gunners",
   "ruling": "Ryan, 2026-09-11: declare the 1934 Reds and Gunners as an exception; hold those 66 rows on the club the short label and both sides of every game agree on. The rule takes the full name because the full name is normally the reliable one; here it demonstrably is not, and following the rule into a known-wrong answer is the letter beating the purpose.",
   "club_seasons": [
    "NFL|1934|club-cincinnati-reds-1933",
    "NFL|1934|club-st-louis-gunners-1934"
   ],
   "men": [
    "P_014383",
    "P_015552",
    "P_015756",
    "P_016023",
    "P_016069",
