    "P_016302",
    "P_016360",
    "P_016421",
    "P_016492",
    "P_016505"
   ],
   "evidence": {
    "what_pfa_prints": "Fifteen men's game-log rows print the club's full name the wrong way round. On the Reds' games (9 Sep - 6 Nov 1934, 7 games) 31 rows read `1934 St. Louis Gunners (NFL) ... CIN`; on the Gunners' games (11 Nov - 2 Dec 1934, 3 games) 35 rows read `1934 Cincinnati Reds (NFL) ... STL`.",
    "what_the_label_says": "The short label on every one of the 66 rows names the other club: CIN on the Reds' games, STL on the Gunners'.",
    "what_the_opponents_say": "In every one of the 10 games, every row of the opposing club -- 153 rows, unanimous -- prints the opponent as the label does: CIN in the Reds' games, STL in the Gunners'.",
    "and_the_schedule": "The Reds played eight games and were suspended; the Gunners took the last three. The dates split exactly there.",
    "no_other_rows_have_this_shape": "Measured 2026-09-11 over all 1,042,006 game claims in model a07168fb425f7339: a row whose short label and the opposing side's printed opponent agree with each other, name no club PFA labels rightly for the row's full-name club, are not the opposing side naming itself, and whose full-name club has no rightly labelled row in that game. Exactly these 66 rows; no other row in the archive. A first, looser definition also caught one 1947 Yankees playoff row labelled NYY -- its own club's rightly labelled rows were in the same game, so it is not this shape.",
    "held_strings": "CIN is what PFA prints for the Reds. PFA's STL for the Gunners names no club the table holds in 1934, so the Gunners' rows sit on the table's code SLG, as the rewritten rows elsewhere do where PFA never prints a resolvable label."
   },
